Network rate control is essential for predictable performance and fairness in HPC and AI workloads, yet existing solutions rely on hardware features or kernel mechanisms requiring privileged access and lack application-semantic awareness. We present PACER, to our knowledge the first userspace framework that integrates semantic-aware rate control directly into the MPI progress engine. PACER enforces bandwidth limits at semantics-safe injection points, preserving both blocking and nonblocking MPI behavior while remaining portable across transport protocols. Our key insight is that rate control inherently introduces structured temporal slack through enforced pacing. Rather than treating this delay as unavoidable overhead, PACER reinterprets it as an opportunity for co-design: pacing-induced slack is converted into adaptive streaming compression, reducing transmitted bytes while maintaining strict rate adherence. We formalize this design as a temporal alignment problem and analytically derive a fixed point for optimal pacing-window selection, enabling principled and adaptive runtime control. Across four representative scenarios and real applications, PACER achieves accurate rate emulation over multiple transports without root access, improves fairness by up to 5.6 × under co-execution, reduces MPI collective latency by up to 72%, and reduces Llama proxy benchmark training time by up to 18% under 25 Gbps constraints.